  "reason": "Patient developed a persistent pattern of excessive goal-directed restlessness and inability to remain idle beginning immediately after a clearly identifiable psychosocial stressor (retirement, 14 months ago). He explicitly denies the cognitive and somatic worry symptoms required for GAD (no muscle tension, no edge/vigilance, no sleep disturbance from worry) and denies euphoric, elevated, or expansive mood, grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, or racing thoughts required for a manic/hypomanic episode, ruling out bipolar disorder. His mood is described as neutral ('in neutral') rather than anxious or depressed. The behavioral disturbance (compulsive puttering, inability to sit still, missed commitments, marital friction) is clearly time-linked to the stressor of retirement and causes clear functional impairment in social and interpersonal domains, without meeting full criteria for another Axis I disorder (GAD, mania, OCD, ADHD adult-onset is atypical). This pattern best fits Adjustment Disorder, unspecified, as the disturbance is behavioral/restlessness-predominant rather than clearly anxious or depressed mood, and is temporally and causally tied to the stressor, persisting due to the ongoing nature of that stressor (chronic retirement adjustment).",
